<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malik-Shah I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1092">1092</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Malik Shah I, Malik Shah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malik Shah I was sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> from 1072 until his death in 1092, when the empire reached its greatest extent. Assisted by his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a>, he presided over a period of strong administration and cultural flourishing. His death was followed by succession struggles that fragmented the Seljuk realm.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i.jpg" alt="Malik-Shah I" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rashid Al-Din · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malik-Shah_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Western Kara-Khanids became vassals of the Seljuks after <b>Malik-Shah I</b> took Samarqand in 1089, and both branches later submitted to the Qara Khitai after the Battle of Qatwan in 1141.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Malik Shah I</b> (r. 1072–1092) the empire campaigned across Syria, Anatolia, the Caucasus and Central Asia and received the submission of the Karakhanids of Samarkand and Kashgar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Kilij Arslan I, held captive by Sultan <b>Malik-Shah</b>, was released after Malik-Shah’s death in 1092 and returned to Nicaea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Threatened by a Fatimid counter-offensive, Atsiz asked <b>Malik-Shah</b> for help.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-atsiz">Emirate of Atsiz</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1085 <b>Malik-Shah</b> sent its former vizier Fakhr al-Dawla ibn Jahir to conquer it, and Diyar Bakr passed to Seljuk governors and later to the Inalids and Artuqids.</blockquote><a href="/state/marwanids">Marwanids</a></li><li><blockquote>He later rebelled against his nephew <b>Malik-Shah</b> and was executed in 1074, but his sons kept Kerman.</blockquote><a href="/state/seljuk-sultanate-of-kerman">Seljuk Sultanate of Kerman</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.68&amp;year=1082" aria-label="Isfahan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5876 977 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Malik-Shah I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6326 1247h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6326 1247h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.68&amp;year=1082">What was here in 1082? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I implored the Lord of Hosts that he would take from me my life and crown, if my brother be more worthy than myself to reign over the Moslems.&#8221;</p><footer>At the tomb of the Imam Riza at Tus, on the eve of battle with his brother (in Gibbon, Decline and Fall, chapter 57) &#183; tr. Edward Gibbon, 1788 &#183; as Gibbon gives it from the Persian historians, answering his vizier &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/735/pg735-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
